# Fixed defect 2390, 2026-11-09, lane b27-place: a `use` naming the folder a
# fetch placed a package in, the lines of the issue, whose program held
# `examples/rpn/`'s catalogue and tree; the parser tells these lines and reads
# neither, so the case holds the lines alone.
#
# SYMPTOM: each line was `use_holds_an_address`, *the catalogue holds that
# address*, which it does not, and advised `use <strconv>/parse` and, nested,
# `use <strconv>/internal/shown` with a `guess` writing it, the module taken
# for the entry: lines the compiler refuses, `unknown_entry`.
#
# CAUSE: `heroes.packages` holds a dot before its `/`, so the line was read as
# Go's host, owner and repository, the folder's last part as the entry.
#
# FIX: the line is told it names the folder a fetch placed a package in, and
# the line reaching the same module through its entry, `use <core>/strconv`,
# with a `guess` writing it, bare and in the mark: only the catalogue says its
# entry `core` placed this folder. A module under the package's `internal`
# folder is told no `use` of the program reaches it, and offered nothing.
#
# The key opens with `2e3`, which the lexer told `exponent_literal` before each
# line until defect 2391: the path is read as a path, never as a number.
use heroes.packages/core@0.1.0-2e3e1bba9c8acea1/strconv  #~ package_unmarked
use heroes.packages/core@0.1.0-2e3e1bba9c8acea1/strconv/internal/shown  #~ package_unmarked
use <heroes.packages/core@0.1.0-2e3e1bba9c8acea1>/strconv as sc  #~ package_unmarked

function main()
    print(strconv.atoi("12").must())
